{
  "abstract": "We construct directed strongly regular graphs with parameters $(108,20,12,2,4)$, $(108,21,13,4,4)$, $(108,25,8,5,6)$, $(108,32,18,8,10)$ and $(108,33,19,10,10)$, and hence with the complementary parameters $(108,87,79,70,70)$, $(108,86,78,68,70)$, $(108,82,65,62,63)$, $(108,75,61,52,52)$ and $(108,74,60,50,52)$. The Hobart–Brouwer table lists all ten as unresolved. All our graphs are Cayley digraphs of the Borel subgroup $B$ of $\\mathrm{SL}(3,3)$, the group of order 108 of upper triangular matrices of determinant 1 over $\\mathbb F_3$. We give eleven connection sets $S$ explicitly and check the identity $S^2=t+\\lambda S+\\mu(B-1-S)$ in $\\mathbb Z[B]$ with two independent scripts. Ten of the sets are invariant under conjugation by $t_0=\\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,-1)$, and each of these graphs has automorphism group $B\\times C_2$, the Borel subgroup of $\\mathrm{GL}(3,3)$. The eleventh, for $(108,25,8,5,6)$, is invariant under the outer automorphism $g\\mapsto w_0g^{-\\mathsf T}w_0$, and its automorphism group is $3^{1+2}_+\\rtimes D_8$. An elementary involutory switching, discussed earlier on the rStation forum and here called the twist, relates rows with $\\lambda=\\mu-2$ to rows with $\\lambda=\\mu$. If $\\tau$ is an involution with $\\tau S\\tau=S$ and $\\tau\\notin S$, and $\\mathrm{Cay}(B,S)$ is a dsrg$(v,k,t,\\mu-2,\\mu)$, then $\\mathrm{Cay}(B,(S\\cup\\{1\\})\\tau)$ is a dsrg$(v,k+1,t+1,\\mu,\\mu)$. It turns $(108,20,12,2,4)$ graphs into $(108,21,13,4,4)$ graphs and $(108,32,18,8,10)$ graphs into $(108,33,19,10,10)$ graphs. It also accounts for the counts of a complete CP-SAT enumeration on the rank-60 action of $B\\times C_2$: 2 isomorphism classes for $(108,20,12,2,4)$ and 6 for $(108,21,13,4,4)$. For $(108,33,19,10,10)$ this action carries at least 22 non-isomorphic graphs. The first graphs came from rerunning, with a time limit of 30 s, action–row pairs that an earlier orbital search had abandoned after 0.05 s: of 200 such pairs, 148 became infeasible and 3 gave graphs.",
